  "_comment": "Per 100g (or per 100ml for liquids). Typical UK supermarket / CoFID figures. Order: [kcal, protein, carbs, fat, fibre].",
  "_convention_state": "Raw weights go with raw values - cooking loses water, not calories. Where a food is used on more than one basis, the basis lives in the key suffix, and the recipe must pick the matching key: _dry (pasta, rice, lentils as weighed from the packet), _cooked (weighed after), _raw, _drained (tin drained, the weight on the can), _made (stock and gravy at drinking strength), _oil (packed in oil, drained). pasta_dry and quinoa_cooked sit in this one table on opposite bases - that is deliberate, not an inconsistency.",
  "_convention_edible": "Values are for the edible portion: root veg peeled, olives pitted, meat off the bone. sweet_potato is flesh only even though the loaded sweet potatoes are eaten skin and all, so that dish under-counts fibre slightly. Recipe grams are bought/prepped weight as the thing hits the pan, so whole veg eaten skin-on multiplies a bought weight by a flesh-only rate and over-counts by roughly the weight of the skin (5-10 percent of that one ingredient). Left as is: it errs high, which is the safe direction here.",
  "_convention_bone": "Bone is never counted as meat. Either use a bone-in key that already prices the bone in (chicken_thigh_bonein), or split the ingredient into a basketOnly line for what is bought and a macroOnly line for what is eaten - see SYSTEM-SPEC.md.",

